The Mage spellbook begins with simple, dependable ideas and becomes stranger as
Arcane rises. Mana Shard and Missile carry the early fighting load. Analyze and
Detect reward curiosity, while Blaze turns an ordinary exit into a thoroughly
immodest departure. Recollection arrives early enough to shape a young Mage's
adventuring rhythm, and the later pages introduce delayed workings, shared
status effects, travel, forbidden research, and finishers built for a group.

Workings appear at these Arcane ranks:

  Arcane 1   Analyze, Blaze, Detect, Mana Shard, Missile
  Arcane 2   Find, Illuminate
  Arcane 3   Fireball, Recollection; Ashkenazy and Falx runes
  Arcane 4   Icestorm, Insane, Investiture; Saloman and Talek runes
  Arcane 5   Teleport; James rune
  Arcane 6   Armor, Echoform, Mage Eye
  Arcane 7   Bolt, Mana Barrier; Rodney rune
  Arcane 8   Levitate, Stasis; Aiken rune
  Arcane 9   Invisibility, Mage Unlock, Necrosis
  Arcane 10  Phase
  Arcane 11  Gate, Glassstorm
  Arcane 12  Inferno
  Arcane 13  Powerup
  Arcane 14  Wrath
  Arcane 15  Singularity, Touch, Grand Mage channel, marginalia

Fireball and Inferno leave enemies Burning. Burning strength may accumulate up
to fifty times an NPC's level, while player targets retain the ordinary
one-times-level cap. Icestorm chills a room, while
Stasis closes a delayed frost equation around one target and applies Freezing.
Glassstorm waits in the air before shattering across nearby enemies and leaving
them Exposed. Singularity is the natural conclusion: it consumes hostile
statuses from the room and gains fifteen percent damage from each condition,
up to seventy-five percent. Echoform approaches the same problem differently,
copying the next direct attack for a smaller strike two seconds later. Necrosis
begins with a blackleaf impact and leaves a short heartbeat-driven disease
behind to keep damaging the target.

Area workings gain some total force as more hostile patterns enter their
geometry, up to twice their one-target total, and still divide that total among
their targets.

Overchannel opens at Arcane 8. "overchannel 5" spends five percent of maximum
SP in addition to the normal cost of each manual damaging working. Arcane 16
opens ten percent and Arcane 32 opens twenty percent. Mastery and Mezari do not
discount the added amount. Use "overchannel off" to return to ordinary casting.
Arcane 500 opens twenty-five percent and Arcane 1000 opens thirty percent.
Spell unlocks end at Arcane 15, but prestige study continues to rank 1000 with
sharply increasing costs and square-root spell scaling beyond rank 32.
Overchannel's added damage also gains half the integer cube root of true Arcane
as an additional damage-per-SP multiplier, so deep ranks make the exchange
more worthwhile.

While any cooldown is active, each heartbeat has a rare chance to revise all
cooldowns away: 1 in 10,000 at low ranks, 25 in 10,000 at Arcane 500, and 100
in 10,000 at Arcane 1000. "mageaura" lists cosmetic prestige auras.

Recollection restores every missing SP after a four-second weave. It costs ten
percent of maximum health and uses a private ten-minute timer, so it does not
occupy ordinary support recovery. Practice shortens that timer; FOCUS and AURA
equipment shorten it further, down to a five-minute floor.

Investiture temporarily borrows one battlemage equipment quality. It can lend
FOCUS to a currently wielded non-FOCUS weapon or write an AURA around the Mage.
The two modes share one effect, so Investiture can complete equipment the Mage
already owns but cannot create full resonance by itself.

Mana Shard, Missile, Fireball, Icestorm, and Bolt have barrage forms. They can
be prepared once Practiced and will then cast lighter versions of themselves
beside ordinary attacks. A wielded FOCUS can prepare a known working while it
is still Copied and pays the automatic cast's SP cost itself. Wearing AURA at
the same time completes battlemage resonance, improving cadence and damage.
These automatic casts continue to teach the spellbook. Missile lands as a
visible two-bolt volley, Fireball leaves a light Burning effect, Icestorm divides its
damage among nearby hostile targets and Chills them, and Bolt emphasizes one
heavier impact. A manually cast Bolt is now a standalone lightning working
with stronger scaling and does not depend on the prepared barrage. A
concentration block pauses barrage cadence without erasing the prepared page;
the cadence resumes where it left off when concentration returns. Silence and
paralysis still collapse it. No barrage can fire more often than every two
combat rounds.

Mana Barrier lasts from forty-five seconds upward and gives each damaging hit
a thirty-five percent chance to spend SP instead of HP after ordinary defenses
have reduced the blow. If SP cannot cover the whole hit, it absorbs as much as
the remaining reserve permits.

Powerup normally converts chosen HP immediately at 175 percent. When Bodach is
bound, it becomes a visible fifteen-pulse buff instead: each pulse spends the
chosen HP amount without taking the Mage below ten HP and restores three times
that amount as SP. Touch has a fourteen-second base cooldown.

Use "spells <working>" or "spellbook <working>" for the details of one page.
"battlemage" reports the exact behavior of your current equipment and barrage.
"overchannel" reports its current percentage and added SP.

Falx shortens a bound working's manual cooldown by twenty percent and also
quickens eligible barrage cadence. Talek, Ravidel, Arcanarton, and Bodach add
their damage as separate ten-percent rune supplements rather than changing the
working's native damage. Each such rune therefore adds damage without making a
native fireball, frost storm, or other working weaker against a resistant foe.
Rodney can only be bound to a duration-bearing buff or control working. It
extends that working's duration by twenty-five percent, including Insane and
Wrath concentration blocks, Stasis freezing, and Bodach Powerup pulses, but
not Burning.
Aiken can only be bound to a damaging working. It uses the established armor
piercing path, halving normal armor absorption while leaving resistance,
intrinsic reduction, shields, and Mana Barrier intact.
Trivlian is the exception to the supplement rule: his pulsing green rune
converts a damaging working's native damage to evil and acid. Its pattern waits
in the Darkwind Mage attic and will only open after Bodach or Arcanarton.
James's Whimsy rune is harmless; Mage Eye sends absurd little scouts, and
Teleport portals acquire colors that responsible geometry would refuse.

Teleport answers to both "tport" and "teleport". The fixed destinations accept
"dw" for Darkwind, "hyper" for Hyperborea, "souv" for Souvrael, and "vi" for
Volcano Isle.
